  note "TRAP. The deadline is a mandatory claim-processing rule, not jurisdictional: Bowles v. Russell rested on 28 U.S.C. § 2107, which covers only civil appeals, and no statute stands behind FRAP 4(b). But once the government objects to untimeliness, the court must dismiss and no equitable exception applies; only the government's failure to raise the objection saves a late appeal. Only 28 U.S.C. § 2255 collateral review — a far harder standard — remains. PLACEHOLDER PAYOFF: $0, override per matter. Conviction stands and appellate review is forfeited by a missed deadline, not by choice -- worse than a deliberate waiver."
